Now I can see the vulnerability. In `SortByPTypeProcess::Execute`, when a mesh has `mPrimitiveTypes == 0`, the code does:

```cpp
if (mesh->mPrimitiveTypes == 0) {
    for (size_t idx = 0; idx < outMeshes.size(); ++idx) {
        delete outMeshes[idx];
    }
    throw DeadlyImportError("Mesh with invalid primitive type: ", mesh->mName.C_Str());
}
```

This deletes all meshes in `outMeshes` and throws. But the meshes in `outMeshes` are also still referenced by `pScene->mMeshes`. When the exception propagates up, the scene destructor will try to delete those meshes again, causing a double-free / use-after-free.

Wait, but let me think more carefully. The `outMeshes` vector contains pointers to meshes. Some of these are original meshes from `pScene->mMeshes` (when `num == 1`), and some are newly created sub-meshes. When we delete them in the error path, we free them. Then when the exception propagates and the scene is destroyed, `aiScene::~aiScene` will try to delete `pScene->mMeshes[i]` again, causing a double-free.

Actually, let me look at this more carefully. The crash is `Heap-use-after-free READ 8` in `aiMesh::~aiMesh`. This suggests that when the scene destructor runs, it tries to read from a mesh that was already freed.

Let me look at the `aiMesh` destructor and `aiScene` destructor to understand the exact mechanism.
